course: dictionary
lesson: 1745
title: Clash: one word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word clash can mean different things, so read the words around it to find the right meaning. Ask: what would fit here? Then swap your meaning into the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
explain: «A family calendar shows everyone what is planned, so events do not clash.» The calendar is about plans and times, so clash here means two things happen at the same time, and you cannot go to both.
explain: In «a clash between fans», clash means a fight or a strong argument. In «a clash of opinions», people's ideas are very different and hard to agree. In music, a clash is a loud metal sound, like cymbals hitting each other.
fact: Context means the words and sentences around a word.
fact: A clash can be a fight or strong argument between people or groups, as in «a clash between fans».
fact: If two events clash, they happen at the same time, so you cannot go to both.
fact: If colours or styles clash, they look bad together, as in «a clash of colours».
fact: A clash can also be a loud metal sound, like cymbals, and a clash of opinions means ideas that are very different and hard to agree.
exercise: «The two football fans had a clash outside the stadium.» What does clash mean here? | *A fight or strong argument | A musical note played softly | A quiet, friendly chat | A new kind of shoe
exercise: The school trip and the football final clash on Friday. What does clash mean? | They are the same colour | They are played loudly on drums | *They happen at the same time, so you cannot go to both | They are very quiet
exercise: «The bright orange top and the pink skirt clash.» What does clash mean? | *They look bad together | They are best friends | They are very old | They make loud sounds
test: The drummer hit the cymbals, and each clash rang out across the hall. What does clash mean here? | A quiet cough | A sad song | A map of the hall | *A loud metal sound
test: «Two people had very different opinions, so their ideas clashed.» Clashed means | Were late for school | *Were very different and hard to agree | Were lost | Agreed on everything
exam: «A family calendar shows everyone what is planned, so events do not clash.» What does the calendar help people avoid? | Forgetting their own names | Loud noises in the house | Arguments about colours | *Two events happening at the same time
